WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.487
Hello! In this video you will learn how to escape a privileged&nbsp;docker container. 

00:00:06.487 --> 00:00:13.940
Before I understood how docker containes work. I thought that they are insanely complicated. They are surely not easy, but they are also 

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:21.290
not that hard. This video will give you an idea about it.&nbsp;
This episode based on a blogpost by Disconnect3d

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:27.680
from Trail of Bits. He's also my friend and he hugely helped my to start by cybersecurity career,

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.169
so make sure to give him a follow! Links, as always, are in the description. Enjoy!

00:00:33.555 --> 00:00:42.680
Docker containers are somewhat similar to virtual machines - each one is better for different use-cases. The difference is, among others,

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.952
that VMs virtualise the hardware and have separate kernel. 

00:00:48.168 --> 00:00:57.690
Containers, on the other hand, are much ligher. On the lower level, they are just processes isolated from the rest of the system.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:02.323
It has many benefits when you want to create an application.

00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:11.300
For example, container will be much lighter than the VM and much easier&nbsp;
to deploy. However, from the security point of

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:17.990
view, it is more risky because there's less isolation between the container and the host operating system.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:27.020
It is possible to configure your container securely.&nbsp;
Then, even if you would have an RCE in the webapp

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.840
that's inside the container, the attacker wouldn't be able to escalate his privileges

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:39.816
to the host machine. However, creating a secure container is not trivial.

00:01:40.248 --> 00:01:47.640
Often, the easiest way to solve some problem is by giving the&nbsp;
container additional privileges.

00:01:48.382 --> 00:01:55.382
In today's episode you see how easy it is to escape a container&nbsp;
that has too much permissions.

00:01:55.382 --> 00:02:04.022
Keep in mind, I am not showing you a docker vulnerability that only works in outdated versions.

00:02:04.022 --> 00:02:13.660
It's a repeatable way to escape the container with specific privileges in the newest version of docker.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:20.560
Precisely, we need to run in the container as root user&nbsp;
and we need the ability to use the mount command.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:29.046
Let's start by explaining control groups or just cgroups&nbsp;
It's a Linux feature

00:02:29.046 --> 00:02:37.540
that allows grouping processes and limiting resources like CPU time&nbsp;
of those groups. It's also one of mechanisms

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:43.638
with which docker isolates processes inside the container from those of the host. 

00:02:43.978 --> 00:02:53.020
And there's a feature that allows to execute a specific command once&nbsp;
all processes in the cgroup are terminated.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:59.920
For example, this can be used to notify the kernel&nbsp;
that all processes in the cgroup are terminated

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.948
and the group can be removed.

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:10.721
This is the feature that will be exploited to escape the container, because this command will be executed on the host.

00:03:11.462 --> 00:03:15.961
Let's jump to the exploit and explain it step by step.

00:03:19.618 --> 00:03:28.683
So we start an ubuntu container. These are the insecure options that will allow us to use the mount command.

00:03:29.193 --> 00:03:34.247
--cap-add is for adding capabilities. Now what are capabilities? 

00:03:34.525 --> 00:03:41.820
Before them, each process would be either non-privileged, subject to all permission checks, or privileged -

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:50.580
efectively run as root, bypassing all kernel permission checks. Capabilities divide those privileges

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:58.890
and allow more granular granting. For example, CHOWN capability allows changing file's owner

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:07.290
while KILL capability allows sending any signals. SYS_ADMIN is a very powerful capability

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:14.160
which will give us the ability to use the mount syscall. Deciding on granting or restricting 

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.700
each capability upon creation of a new container would be very time-consuming. This problem is

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:29.545
partially solved by AppArmor. Among other things, AppArmor can specify a list of restricted capabilities.

00:04:29.545 --> 00:04:36.030
This command turns off docker-default AppArmor profile, because it restricts

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.334
the SYS_ADMIN capability which we need.

00:04:39.507 --> 00:04:44.610
Running this command gives us a shell inside the container

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:49.769
and our goal is to execute a command on the host.

00:04:49.769 --> 00:05:01.620
The first step of the exploit is creating a new cgroup and, using mount command, telling de system&nbsp;
where are the files that describe this cgroup.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:07.803
Because in practice, cgroup is just a bunch of files and this is its structure.

00:05:08.524 --> 00:05:14.846
There's one file particularly interesting to us - notify_on_release.

00:05:14.846 --> 00:05:18.577
When the value inside this file is 0

00:05:18.757 --> 00:05:23.252
nothing happens when all processes from the cgroup are terminated.

00:05:23.252 --> 00:05:34.157
However, when the value is 1, upon terminating all processes, a command from release_agent file from upper directory is executed.

00:05:34.157 --> 00:05:43.390
So in our exploit, we put the value 1 inside notify_on_release file in our cgroup.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:50.796
The command that we will want to execute will be put in&nbsp;the /cmd file inside the container.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.734
If our attack will be successful, this file will be executed by the host.

00:05:57.734 --> 00:06:09.585
However, if we run /cmd on the host, there will be "No such file" error, because the host won't know that this file is inside the container.

00:06:09.585 --> 00:06:16.760
And obviously, being in the container we can't just write a file on the host OS.

00:06:17.366 --> 00:06:25.258
So we need to find under what path files from inside the container are reachable on the host.

00:06:25.258 --> 00:06:33.718
For this, we need to look in the /etc/mtab file. And we'll use a handy regex to extract this path.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:39.393
Next, we put this path inside the release_agent file.

00:06:39.393 --> 00:06:46.151
Then, we put our command that will be executed on the host inside this /cmd file.

00:06:46.151 --> 00:06:56.533
In this case, we'll just list processes of the host and put the result inside the /output file inside the container so we can read it later. 

00:06:57.386 --> 00:07:03.798
So for this whole thing to be triggered, all processes from the cgroup must be terminated.

00:07:03.798 --> 00:07:07.423
But there are no processes in the cgroup yet.

00:07:07.423 --> 00:07:15.882
To add process to the cgroup, we need to put this process' id inside the cgroup.procs file.

00:07:15.882 --> 00:07:24.935
So we create a new process and all it does is putting its PID inside this cgroup.procs file.

00:07:24.935 --> 00:07:31.460
$$ is a special bash variable that contains each process' own id.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:40.405
And that process is terminated just after doing that. And that's the moment when the release_agent is executed.

00:07:40.979 --> 00:07:45.530
And in this case, the release_agent is out /cmd file.

00:07:45.743 --> 00:07:54.325
So on the host the ps command is executed and the result is put inside the /output file inside the container.

00:07:54.325 --> 00:08:03.764
We can then cat this file and see the result which proves that the attack was successful and we were able to escape the container.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:11.096
To prevent such attack from happening, when creating a docker container, always remember

00:08:11.096 --> 00:08:19.100
to avoid using --privileged flag, because is basically gives the container full access to the host.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:26.054
Also, never mount docker socket inside the container, because then, the attacker would be able

00:08:26.054 --> 00:08:30.630
to create a new privileged container himself and use this technique.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:39.551
And also, never run as root inside the container, because it opens a lot of potential attack vectors to escalate privileges.

00:08:39.551 --> 00:08:45.122
Because keep in mind that the root inside the container is not some different root.

00:08:45.122 --> 00:08:50.252
By default, its exactly the same user as root on the host.

00:08:50.252 --> 00:08:56.251
I hope that after watching the video, you understand container escapes a little bit better.

